TAM-IDOL - THE EXEMPLAR TAMLINUX REPOSITORY

tam-idol is the model every Tamlinux repository copies. It holds the
standard set of files and folders, the text that goes in each folder's
README, and the procedures every project follows. When a repository
does something differently, either the repository changes or tam-idol
does, so that there is one way of working across all our projects.

tam-idol has no program in it. Its version (VERSION) is the version of
the standard, and each repository's README says which version it
follows, for example:

  Follows the tam-idol 0.1.0 standard.

tools/idol-check reports what a repository is missing.


THE STANDARD SET

  Every repository has these:

  README            plain text: what the project is, how to build and
                    use it, WHERE THINGS ARE, and the tam-idol version
  COPYRIGHT         copyright and license notices, including those of
                    code taken from other projects
  LICENSE           the license text (GNU GPL, version 3)
  VERSION           the version, one line, for example 0.0.1
  ROADMAP.md        milestones with their version numbers
  UPSTREAM.md       every piece of code taken from another project
  AI-BLAME.md       how this repository records which AI did what
  .gitignore        build output and nothing else

  arch/             architecture: how the code is built, and why
  bugs/             one record per bug: B1, B2, ...
  ideas/            ideas from other projects, one file per project
  plans/            one plan per work phase: P1, P2, ...
  reviews/          one record per review: R1, R2, ...
  work/             WHATNOW, and one record per work session: W1, W2,
                    ...

  Every folder has a README-<folder> (README-arch, README-bugs, ...)
  that says what goes in it and how. The text above its IN THIS
  REPOSITORY section is copied from tam-idol unchanged; the section
  itself describes the folder's contents in that repository.

  A repository with code adds:

  Makefile          make, make test, make install, make uninstall,
                    make clean; PREFIX defaults to $HOME/.local
  src/, include/    the source
  tests/            the tests that make test runs
  tools/            helper scripts for developing the project

  ideas/sources and tools/pull-references are standard wherever a
  repository keeps local clones of the projects it learns from.

  arch/layout.md lists the set with the reason for each entry.


THE PROCEDURES

  Starting and ending a session. Read work/WHATNOW first: its NEXT
  section is the to-do list. At the end, write one record, W<n>-<name>,
  and revise WHATNOW as a whole, and commit both with the work.
  work/README-work has the formats.

  Bugs. One file per bug, B<n>-<name>.md, with a status and a severity.
  A number is never reused. bugs/README-bugs.

  Plans. One plan per work phase, P<n>-<name>.md, covering a range of
  versions from ROADMAP.md. Fred's decisions go into the plan with
  their date; until then a remark from Fred is context, not a decision.
  plans/README-plans.

  Reviews. One file per review, R<n>-<name>.md, naming the exact commit
  examined. A confirmed finding becomes a bug. A review is evidence to
  check, not permission to ship. reviews/README-reviews.

  Ideas. One file per project we learn from, with a verdict on each
  idea: adopt, consider or reject. Code taken, not just an idea, is also
  recorded in UPSTREAM.md and COPYRIGHT. ideas/README-ideas.

  Architecture. arch/ describes the code as it is. A change that alters
  a structure, an algorithm or a dependency updates arch/ in the same
  commit. Where code and document disagree, the document is the bug.
  arch/README-arch.

  Versions. Every code change bumps VERSION in the same commit: the
  minor number for a feature, the patch number for a fix. The program's
  --version prints it, so anyone can see which build they run.

  Commits. Commit only the paths you changed (git commit -- <paths>),
  never git add -A or git commit -a. Commits made with AI help carry
  Co-authored-by, AI-Tool (with the version) and AI-Model trailers.
  Never amend a commit that may have been pushed.

  Branches. Work happens on dev, and agents push dev freely. main, tags
  and releases move only on Fred's word.

  Privacy. These repositories are public. Nothing in them names a
  session ID, a transcript, a home-directory path, a private repository
  or a private decision number. Fred is "Fred"; his full name appears
  only in COPYRIGHT.

  Writing. Plain words. README and every README-<folder>, the work
  records and WHATNOW are plain text, lines no longer than 76
  characters, section names in capitals. The .md files are Markdown
  that still reads well as plain text. No emojis.


STARTING A NEW REPOSITORY

  1. git init -b dev in ~/Code/tamlinux/<name>.
  2. Copy from tam-idol: COPYRIGHT, LICENSE, AI-BLAME.md, UPSTREAM.md,
     .gitignore, and every folder's README-<folder>. Copy
     work/README-work and tools/pull-references with them.
  3. Replace "tam-idol" with the project's name in COPYRIGHT and
     AI-BLAME.md, and rewrite each IN THIS REPOSITORY section.
  4. Write README, VERSION (0.0.0 until there is code) and ROADMAP.md.
  5. Write work/WHATNOW and work/W1-<name> for the session.
  6. Run tools/idol-check on the new repository; it must pass.


BRINGING A REPOSITORY UP TO STANDARD

  1. Run tools/idol-check <repository>. It lists what is missing and
     which README-<folder> files differ from tam-idol's text.
  2. Add what is missing and bring each README-<folder> into line,
     moving the repository's own content into IN THIS REPOSITORY.
  3. Record the change in a W record and in WHATNOW, and say in README
     which tam-idol version the repository follows.

  Changing the standard itself starts here: change tam-idol, bump its
  VERSION, and note the change in ROADMAP.md. Repositories catch up when
  they are next worked on.


WHERE THINGS ARE

  arch/            why the standard is the way it is, and its layout
  bugs/            defects in the standard or in idol-check
  ideas/           conventions of other projects we learn from
  plans/           the plan to bring every repository up to standard
  reviews/         reviews of the standard
  work/            WHATNOW (read it first) and the work records
  tools/           idol-check and pull-references
  ROADMAP.md       versions of the standard and the repositories to do
  UPSTREAM.md      code taken from other projects (none)
  AI-BLAME.md      how AI work is recorded here
  COPYRIGHT        copyright and license notices
  LICENSE          GNU General Public License, version 3


